ory:** compute the vacancy formation energy by relaxation and the migration barrier by a nudged-elastic-band calculation, then plug them into a transition-state-theory formula at 300 K.\n- **(b) High-temperature MD with a vacancy:** run fcc copper containing one vacancy at about 900–1300 K, fit an Arrhenius law and report the activation energy and prefactor. Any extrapolation to 300 K would be flagged as such.\n- **(c) Liquid copper just above melting (~1400 K):** MD can measure this directly (around 1e-5 cm²/s) with a block-averaged uncertainty.\n\nFor (a) and (b), the copper melting point in the calibration file (1357.77 K) can serve as a sanity check on the model. Tell me which alternative you want and I'll run it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1969,"cache_read_input_tokens":20988,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1969,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74e6e914-0deb-4991-a0f5-7f18d2e79c81","uuid":"64343c3c-4a28-4088-8c5a-4943b34f4dfe","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:15:03.437Z","request_id":"req_011CgbF6Mjv9Du9dXpWUZUEB"}
